<component type="desktop-application">
  <id>io.github.kennyporto.Handset</id>
  <name>Handset</name>
  <summary>Answer your phone calls on your PC</summary>
  <metadata_license>CC0-1.0</metadata_license>
  <project_license>MIT</project_license>
  <developer id="io.github.kennyporto">
    <name>KennyPorto</name>
  </developer>

  <description>
    <p>
      Handset turns your PC into a hands-free kit for your phone, the same way a car does.
      When your phone rings, answer it on the PC and talk through your headset or speakers
      while the phone stays in your pocket.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Answer or decline right from the notification</li>
      <li>Place calls with the built-in dial pad</li>
      <li>See who's calling, and call your favourites, recent calls or anyone in your contacts, if your phone shares them</li>
      <li>Mute your microphone and send keypad tones during a call</li>
      <li>Handle a second incoming call while you're on the first</li>
      <li>Runs quietly in the system tray, and can start when you log in</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Handset connects to your phone for calls only, so music and videos keep playing on the
      phone. It works with any phone that supports Bluetooth hands-free, Android or iPhone.
    </p>
    <p>
      Your computer needs Bluetooth and PipeWire 1.4 or newer. Pair your
      phone in the Bluetooth settings before you start.
    </p>
    <p>
      This package installs the unmodified Handset build from Handset's GitHub releases at
      install time. It runs on the KDE runtime, which provides Qt and Kirigami.
    </p>
  </description>

